{"status":"ready","summary":"I changed one line in src/optimizer/join_filter_pushdown_optimizer.cpp. When a hash join's build side is a LOGICAL_DELIM_GET, build_side_has_filter is now true. Before, it was set by IsFiltering(join.children[0]), which tests the probe side, so a bare probe-side scan never got a Bloom filter. The runtime gates in CanUseBloomFilter still apply: the comparison must be COMPARE_EQUAL, the hash table must not be perfect, and the probe estimate must be larger than the build count. The selectivity check that switches off an unselective filter also still applies. The join-type gating in GenerateJoinFilters (no MARK/SINGLE/LEFT/OUTER/ANTI) is unchanged.

Measured on q22: the orders scan in the decorrelated NOT EXISTS now has `o_custkey IN BF(c_custkey)` and emits 1.9M rows instead of 15M. Bench gave q22 -23.6%, -26.7%, -19.8% and -18.9% over four runs. Results matched in every bench run.

q17, the target in the issue, did not improve: +1.0%, -4.3%, +18.7%, -1.3%, which is noise. I did not check why. My guess is that its DELIM-build join uses a perfect hash table, which CanUseBloomFilter refuses on purpose. q02 and q04 went both ways between runs (q02 -16%, +7%, +22%, -1%), so they are noise. The machine was very noisy: q03, which has no DELIM join, read +25% and +31% in two runs and -4% in a third. Geomean was -0.1% on the full run, +12% on a noisy subset and -6.6% on the last subset.","generality":"This helps decorrelated correlated subqueries: scalar aggregates, and EXISTS/IN rewritten as DELIM joins. It applies when the subquery's inner join probes a large table on the correlated key against the DELIM_GET of distinct outer values. Those values are usually a small subset of the key domain, so the Bloom filter prunes most of the probe-side scan. It does not apply to MARK/ANTI/LEFT/SINGLE joins (unchanged gating), to non-equality conditions, or when the build becomes a perfect hash table. If the delim set covers the whole domain, the filter's selectivity check turns it off after the first vectors.","notes":"The issue predicted a q17 gain, but none showed up. Its subquery join (lineitem against the DELIM_GET of about 2K part keys) probably uses a perfect or sparse-perfect hash table, and CanUseBloomFilter returns false when is_perfect_hashtable is set. A follow-up could allow the exact-bitmap BF for perfect hash tables when the min/max range is sparse compared with the build count, but earlier memory notes ([[bf-sparse-perfect-hj-no-gain]]) found that unhelpful on q21. Benchmark noise was extreme (±30% on untouched templates), so re-run subsets before trusting any single run."}
